---
import DocPage from '../components/DocPage.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/catalog';
---

<DocPage
  title="How fresh are these prices?"
  lede="What verified, stale, imported, seed and estimated mean, and who is allowed to set them."
  path="/how-fresh/"
>
  <p>
    <code>prices_checked</code> is set by a person. A maintainer confirms a rate against the provider's own pricing page
    and writes that date into the provider YAML. The freshness bot never writes it, not on a drift fix and not on a match.
    At build time the date is copied into <code>provenance.last_verified</code>, which every signal below is derived from.
  </p>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <dl class="legend">
    <div><dt><span class="status" data-tone="ok">verified</span></dt><dd><code>last_verified</code> is present and within the provider's <code>staleness_threshold_days</code> (60 unless the provider sets its own).</dd></div>
    <div><dt><span class="status" data-tone="warn">stale</span></dt><dd><code>last_verified</code> is present but older than the threshold. Confirmed once, possibly moved since.</dd></div>
    <div><dt><span class="status" data-tone="muted">imported</span></dt><dd>Never confirmed by a person. The rate came from another catalog.</dd></div>
    <div><dt><span class="status" data-tone="muted">seed</span></dt><dd>Never confirmed by a person. Bootstrap data with no import origin.</dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p>
    <code>imported</code> and <code>seed</code> are never reported as stale: stale implies the price was verified at some
    point. On this site, stale is worked out in your browser against today's date, so a page built weeks ago still tells
    the truth about age. The Python package does the same at load time with <code>model_freshness</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Estimated rates</h2>
  <p>
    Status answers <em>is this rate current</em>. It does not answer <em>is this rate the meter</em>. Some vendors publish
    two numbers for one model in two units and bill on only one of them. OpenAI prices <code>gpt-4o-transcribe</code> per
    token and also prints a per-minute figure it heads "Estimated cost". Where the catalog carries such a number, the row
    is marked <span class="tag" data-marker="estimated">estimated</span>: useful to compare vendors and plan capacity, not
    to reconcile a bill.
  </p>

  <h2>How LLM rates are checked</h2>
  <p>
    LLM rates are cross-checked against four external aggregators: Helicone, OpenRouter, LiteLLM, and Simon Willison's
    llm-prices, and each disagreement is resolved by hand. LLM rows carry no per-model status for this reason; a status
    there would misread as low confidence.
  </p>

  <h2>How voice rates are checked</h2>
  <p>
    The aggregators do not carry speech models, so voice rates are re-read from each model's own pricing page. A
    maintainer-triggered job renders the page in a headless browser and an LLM extracts the current rate. Deterministic
    guards run before anything is proposed: the quote must appear on the page, the number must match the quote, the row,
    currency and unit must be right, and no implausible jump is allowed. When a rate has drifted, the job opens one rolling
    pull request, and a person reviews every change and sets <code>prices_checked</code> on merge. Nothing is merged
    automatically.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rates marked <span class="tag" data-marker="api">api</span> come from a vendor endpoint that serves prices as data, and
    are re-read on a schedule rather than by a person reading a page.
  </p>

  <h2>Staying current in code</h2>
  <p>
    The price data ships inside the installed package, so it ages with your pinned version. <code>UpdatePrices</code>
    refreshes it at runtime, opt-in, from the same <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/prices/data.json`} rel="noopener">data.json</a>
    this site is built from.
  </p>
</DocPage>
